{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-35835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-35835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{5{x}^{7}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-5{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(10+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{4}+0}{10+0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-35835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e6efbab36c4cc15ca5a9a199e2d7fe3b8275fcf97866a9e0075dd16a2e1a6af"} {"question" "q-35835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-35835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2"] ["math" "y=-5"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=0"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-35835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "No horizontal asymptote"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e6efbab36c4cc15ca5a9a199e2d7fe3b8275fcf97866a9e0075dd16a2e1a6af"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\infty "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-78008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-101316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-101316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+7-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-101316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e6efbab36c4cc15ca5a9a199e2d7fe3b8275fcf97866a9e0075dd16a2e1a6af"} {"question" "q-101316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-101316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-101316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-106705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|{x}^{3}-5|}{2-{x}^{3}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-106705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|{x}^{3}-5|=({x}^{3}-5).$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|{x}^{3}-5|}{2-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-5}{2-{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-5}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(1-\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-106705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e6efbab36c4cc15ca5a9a199e2d7fe3b8275fcf97866a9e0075dd16a2e1a6af"} {"question" "q-106705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-106705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1"] ["math" "y=-3"] ["math" "y=1"] ["math" "y=2"] ["text" "No horizontal asymptote"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-106705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-302240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x<0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-302240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|8x-7|=-(8x-7).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{7}{x})}{(\\frac{-5x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8-\\frac{7}{x})}{(-5+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(8-0)}{-5+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{8}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-302240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e6efbab36c4cc15ca5a9a199e2d7fe3b8275fcf97866a9e0075dd16a2e1a6af"} {"question" "q-302240" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"} {"question" "q-302240" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979274/state.json" "file_sha256" "3ccd0e0bee87537c927c8b7750821fc1ebfff93ba7edd026d66f0fe1e361131c"}] "usage" {} "basis" 856 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" 